ows!\n\n\n**Discovering Column Pattern**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the column-wise transformations. Analyzing the input and output columns, I'm noting significant patterns. Specifically, I'm converting blocks [2,0] into 0 and [2,2] to 1. I'm focusing on how these blocks shift positions within columns. It seems like the output is derived from the input's column arrangement.\n\n\n**Verifying Column Sorting**\n\nI've confirmed the column sorting mechanism! Output column order directly corresponds to the ascending counts of '1's in the input columns. This applies consistently across test cases. For Train 2, I'm now examining the '3' counts in the odd rows, verifying this crucial sorting rule.\n\n\n**Verifying Column Sorting with Train 2**\n\nI've verified the column sorting mechanism using Train 2's data. I've re-examined the column-wise transformations, confirming that output column order aligns perfectly with the ascending counts of '1's (solid blocks) in the input columns. This holds true for rows 7, 9 and 11, across the whole grid. This mechanism works consistently.\n\n\n",
